\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Layer merging in the Whisper decoder: each student layer is a weighted average of two consecutive teacher layers, then trained with cross-entropy and knowledge distillation.}}{72}{figure.caption.72}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Head shearing of the Whisper embedding through low-rank decomposition. The decomposition is activation-aware because the low-rank weights are subsequently feature-distilled.}}{74}{figure.caption.78}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Choice of \(\alpha \) and \(\beta \). Bayesian hyperparameter search indicates that the main constraint is to keep the lower-layer weight \(\alpha \) small.}}{77}{figure.caption.87}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Activation similarities between all decoder-layer pairs. Consecutive layers tend to be more similar, suggesting that they can be merged.}}{78}{figure.caption.89}%
